! nalu-engine — Invariant

!! Identity

`nalu-engine` is a TW5 `module-type: startup` module written in TS, compiled into the
plugin bundle, and evaluated inside the wiki at boot. It installs the `$tw.lares.*` API
that the TS-side `IslandAdaptor` calls across the shore.

The wiki IS the reactive engine (yin-collapse law, lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/nalu).
This module lands the nalu wave in the wiki. It answers HOW one change lands; the wiki shore answers
WHEN (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/outbound-bridge`).

!! $tw.lares.* surface

| Method | Caller | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `applyNalu(change)` | the wiki shore (`IslandAdaptor`), once per landed change | Land ONE change synchronously: a tombstone or an absent record deletes the title; any other record adds the tiddler `toWikiFields` builds from the envelope |
| `isApplyingNalu()` | a synchronous caller | True while an `applyNalu` call runs |

`$tw.lares.enqueueNalu` belongs to the wiki shore. The shore installs its queue door there and removes
it at teardown; this module never holds one.

!! Position in the stack

```
TS shore (irreducible — Automerge, network, crypto, storage)
  AutomergeDocStore → MemeProvider → projection bus → IslandAdaptor.onUriChanged
  the landing queue (WaveLanding) → IslandAdaptor lands one change
                              → $tw.lares.applyNalu(change)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
TW5 startup module (this spec)
  applyNalu(change)                       ← wiki.addTiddler / wiki.deleteTiddler
  _applying flag                          ← read through isApplyingNalu()
```

!! Invariants

''N-1 One applier per wiki.''
The module holds one wiki and one `_applying` flag at module level. Every inbound change enters the
shore's queue, whatever its origin kind; the shore calls `applyNalu` when the value it resolved
differs from the value the wiki holds, and every such write passes through this one applier.

''N-2 One change, one synchronous stretch.''
`applyNalu` lands exactly one change and returns. The engine holds no queue, no timer and no frame,
and it wraps nothing in a transaction: TW5 gathers changed titles until its own scheduled
`nextTick` dispatch, and that dispatch delivers the nalu to the widgets. The dispatch boundary
follows TW5's scheduling, never the shore's slices.

''N-3 The queue belongs to the shore, one shore per engine.''
The wiki shore owns the ordered queue, its slices, the host-task pacing between them and the seed
checkpoint. It installs its door only where `applyNalu` already stands (else
`LandingDoorUnavailableError`), and a second shore on an engine whose door stands occupied refuses by
`LandingDoorOccupiedError`.

''N-4 The apply flag covers the call, never the dispatch.''
`_applying` reads `true` for the length of one `applyNalu` call and returns to the value it found in a
`finally`, so an apply that begins inside another leaves the outer one raised. TW5 dispatches `change`
on `nextTick`, after the flag lowered, so a `change` listener never reads it raised. The wiki shore
therefore tells a wave from a hand by WHAT STANDS: its landed ledger holds the value each wave left
under each title, and a notification that repeats that value reads as foam
([[outbound-bridge|lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/outbound-bridge]] #/the-shore-law).

''N-5 Tiddler fields = record.tiddler + title + `$origin-bag`.''
For a non-tombstone change, the engine builds the wiki fields from `{ ...change.record.tiddler }`,
restores `title` from `change.title`, and stamps `$origin-bag` from `change.bag` when the envelope
names one. `bag` stays the author's own field and rides through untouched.
For a tombstone (`record === null` or `record.meta?.deleted === true`), the engine
calls `wiki.deleteTiddler(change.title)`.

''N-6 No wiki, no apply.''
An `applyNalu` call with no wiki under it throws `NaluWikiUnavailableError`, which names the title.

''N-7 Idempotent startup.''
`startup()` reads `$tw.lares ?? {}`, installs the two functions, writes back. Safe to
run more than once in tests; subsequent invocations overwrite with identical refs.

!! The seed

The recipe (`buildIslandRecipe`) installs the shore's landing queue, then drives
`emitInitialReplay()` per bag: every existing record enters that queue as an ordinary change. The
shore's seed checkpoint resolves once its queue and its tracked work settle (a seed landing that
fails twice rejects it), and the kernel awaits it before it arms the
island's behavior. Seed and live changes share one road; the engine sees no difference between them.

<<~ ahu slot=`#/gate-families-shore`>>

!! The gate-families shore ~ runtime ≠ physics

ONE shore queue holds the changes of every bag (N-3). Upstream,
`projection-nalu` (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/projection-nalu`) stands a `KeyedCoalesceGate`
''per bag''. Both stand; they sit at OPPOSITE ENDS of the one shape `SOURCE → gate → SINK`, never
the same layer.

<<~ scale name=`shore` ranks=`UPSTREAM/provider-coalesce-per-key -> DOWNSTREAM/shore-landing-queue-per-slice -> ENGINE/apply-one`>>

* ''UPSTREAM — the provider gates (physics).'' `MemeProvider` (the TS-side, before the
  wiki shore) draws the coalesce gates: a per-URI debounce collapses the replay patches a fresh
  vessel-connect fires for one URI down to ONE latest-state change, and the `_changesetGate`
  (`meme-provider.ts`) coalesces a storm of large Automerge transactions to ONE
  `onChangeset(union)` per bag. This names the ''coalesce'' family — intermediates lawfully DROP,
  newest-per-key wins. The drop happens HERE, before a change ever reaches the landing queue.
* ''DOWNSTREAM — the landing queue (runtime).'' Each surviving change reaches
  the island adaptor and joins the shore's ONE queue in arrival order, a changeset as one family. The
  shore processes whole families, up to 200 queued changes per slice, and yields to the host between slices; each
  landing resolves its title through the composition, and the shore calls `applyNalu` when that
  value differs from the value the wiki holds. A landing that fails twice stays owed for a later
  wave; a standing shore coalesces NOTHING further.

So "one queue, all bags" governs the DELIVERY runtime — the shore refuses to re-shard its queue per
bag. The per-key coalesce gates do not contradict it, because they live UPSTREAM, shaping the stream
BEFORE it reaches the queue. ''The shore and the engine carry the runtime; the gate-families carry the''
''physics it enacts'' (`role = capability ≠ platform`). The three compose end-to-end:
coalesce-per-key (drop intermediates) → one queue (preserve order) → apply one (land). WITNESSED: the
applier (`modules/nalu-engine.ts`) + the queue (`wave-landing.ts`, `island-adaptor.ts`) + the provider
gates (`mesh/meme-provider.ts`, `mesh/projection-nalu.ts`).

